 > What is a "Windows 7 Beta"?

Microsoft, having released an operating system
upgrade of MS-Windows XP, called "Vista", that was
underwhelmingly accepted by the user community, has
rushed to release what is hopefully (by them) a less
objectionable system with a name while in
development of "Windows 7". Persons with the right
chops to wring out that system while it is still in
development have been given "beta release", a fancy
name for "test version". copies with which to
interact and report both problems and kudos.

Knowing what I know as a software developer since
1961 about "rushed to release", especially with
highly complex software systems, whatever "Windows
7" is called when it hits the store shelves is going
to be a world class "bug-nest for the ages".

This is also consistent with Microsoft's reputation
for terrible software quality, one of the base
causes of there being a long-existing Alter-Net
newsgroup called "alt.destroy.microsoft".

Microsoft is currently mulling shedding a lot of
staff in these hard economic times, so their failure
to keep a reputation as a vendor of highest quality
products may be coming home to roost.

 > I assume "Windows 7 Beta" runs on an Atom with 1.5
 > GHz, 1.997 GB of memory, while Nethack consumes
 > 0.1 GHz, 3 MB of memory?

Which does remind me, of using, back in 1974, a DEC
PDP 11/23 computer whose entire operating system fit
in 4 kilobytes, a 2 kilobyte "root" portion that did
overlays as needed, and a 2 kilobyte "overlay"
portion that did the real work.

NetHack has become a bit of a resource hog, but if
rewritten into an overlaid system, several levels
deep, could probably run very comfortably in the
fraction of a 40 kilobyte computer system some "near
bare metal" 4 kilobyte operating system didn't
consume.

Doing that is only an exercise for the
pathologically bored, but there's no reason, in
concept, that doing it should not be possible.

The thing is, with ever growing computer hardware
platform capabilities, use of software overlays has
quite a bit gone out of style today, though I'm
betting there are some embedded computer systems
that still use them with overlays read from
firmware instead of from disk or tape.

